[Music]  i  am going to be discussing today my new  book the corona crash  which comes out on the 27th of october  and it really builds on the themes  of uh my previous books stolen how to  save the world from financialization  which i presented  um not so long ago it must be about six  months ago at uh how the light gets in  earlier this year  um but that rook book really looked at  the kind of pre-existing problems that  our economy was facing even before the  pandemic hit  so stolen was really the story of the  rise and fall of finance-led growth  which i described as this  um particular kind of political economic  regime  that governed our economy uh the  government the uk's economy the us's  economy broadly speaking the global  economy to a greater or lesser extent in  different places  um really kind of from the 1980s onwards  and it was characterized by um you know  what a lot of people refer to as kind of  neoliberal ideology when it comes to  governance  so regulation cutting taxes uh financial  deregulation in particular  uh privatization a general retreat of  the state from  providing a lot of the kind of uh  services that we previously relied upon  the state to provide um that uh  gross model i argue and stolen um was  premised upon  a couple of different elements that made  it pretty unstable  so financialization um which was the  kind of process at the core of finance  led growth  drove a lot of different changes in the  way our economy works and in the book i  look at how  uh the firm the household and the state  all became  financialized and i also talked a little  bit about how the global economy  became for more uh financialized and  really what that means  is that um kind of financial  institutions so  banks hedge funds pension funds etc um  and  financial kind of motives and operations  more generally so that's really the kind  of logic of  lending and investment become much more  prominent in  uh basically all areas of the economy so  you had the financialization of the  corporation  which was associated with the kind of  dominance of the ideology of shareholder  value  which basically meant that corporations  came to focus pretty much solely on  their their share prices  um rather than you know what we might  have  previously thought of as a kind of  stakeholder model of corporate  governance or even you know rather than  actually  um doing what many of these corporations  were built to do which was kind of make  things and  provide services there was a kind of  singular focus on this on this idea of  maximizing shareholder value and the  ways in which that was pursued so  particularly um excessive dividends  payments  um increasing kind of mergers and  acquisitions activity  share buybacks for example uh  general reorganization of power that  took place within the firm so  workers wages were much were lowered  relative to the wages  and perks that were gained by senior  executives  and obviously the ratio of pay between  the average worker and ceos  increases substantially over that period  um and that  really created a lot of the problems  that we see with corporations today so  there are those kind of problems of  governance the fact that  um stakeholders the fact that workers  the environment aren't really big  factors in a lot of corporations  decision making but also some like  more kind of structural economic factors  so the fact that we've seen a kind of  long-term decline in investment  uh in fixed capital amongst corporations  so you know  a corporation was less likely to say  build a new factory which would have  created  jobs or you know invest in research and  development and it would be to kind of  basically play around with its share  price so that has created all sorts of  problems around productivity  and at the same time obviously we had  this internal redistribution of power  which led to  a falling wage growth and has led to i  mean then this has happened  across the kind of rich world a fall in  the wage share of national income a rise  in the profit share of national income  that brings us on to how it affected the  household so you had a massive rise  in um in borrowing um and a lot of that  was mortgage borrowing because obviously  at the same time as these changes were  coming in  um you had a big housing boom you had  the deregulation of commercial banking  which made it much easier people to get  mortgages and to get debt more generally  and you obviously also had right to buy  so a lot of people were kind of buying  um using this newly created credit to  buy housing  and that as we know was explained in the  book drives this uh this bubble that we  see in the housing market in the uk and  then eventually  the us which ultimately obviously brings  the whole system  crashing down and then you have the  financialization of the state  which was associated with um the kind of  incursion  of private financial institutions into  the operation of  uh the management of public services the  provision of  infrastructure you can see this with the  the private financing initiatives but  also  with outsourcing more generally which is  of course very much coming back to bite  during this pandemic when outsourcing  companies are basically being charged  with running the government's corona  iris response  and not doing so very successfully at  all  um so yeah and it's also associated with  actually the rise of this idea of  asset-based welfare which is that rather  than  um kind of socializing risk throughout  society risk is increasingly  individualized so  people are encouraged to have private  pensions to store up  savings in their home and that's really  what's going to be what's going to  protect you from any negative shocks in  your life rather than  the state itself so these are all  factors that are associated with  financialization  and kind of as i argue in the book and  as i write at the beginning of this book  those processes severely weakened both  the uk economy and the global economy  and the run-up to the pandemic  obviously we have the financial crisis  which kind of brings the  energy the dynamism that exists within  that model to a halt a lot of the  productivity gains that we saw  pre-financial crisis were driven by kind  of financial innovation which is  just a euphemism for finding a more  ingenious way just to uh extract money  um from uh i mean ultimately it was it  was mortgage  holders but also various other areas of  the economy um  so that obviously you know came came  crashing down um but you also had these  secular problems within corporations  where we had  an underlying trend away from kind of  investment in  making things and kind of providing uh  services um that were innovative and  investing in research and development  and those sorts of things and towards  much more speculative activities at  least amongst many corporations in the  global north  as well as because of these trends  towards uh  well i mean obviously increasing  shareholder value the best way to do  that  really is to kind of corner a particular  market and gain a monopoly position so  that was another way that firms have  done that and  we saw a steady increase in monopoly  power over that period  and ultimately those are the trends that  ultimately gave rise to  that period that we've had since the  financial crisis which was characterized  above all by massive stagnation so there  were those kind of  underlying trends that were making the  economy less dynamic  but also you know there was this huge  buildup of debt before the financial  crisis  and then after the financial crisis that  debt had to be repaid and that was  basically you know the growth we had  before the financial crisis  was uh at the expense of future growth  because that's really what that is if  you're not using debt for investment  then you are simply taking your future  earnings or your future income  and having it now and that's really a  bit another big reason why we saw a lack  of dynamism in the  economy since 2008 because obviously  we've had a decade of  wage stagnation a decade of productivity  stagnation  poverty and inequality um increasing in  some areas of the economy inequality  absolutely increasing we now  know that we've been systematically  understating the extent of inequality in  the economy because of the capacity of  many individuals to hide  income uh as you know uh wealth  basically  income from work as income from other  sources or to hide income  as actual uh physical wealth which has  prevented us from accurately  understanding the scale of inequality in  this country but some researchers have  actually just done some research that  shows that it has grown quite  substantially  so you know we had uh low productivity  low levels of investment  big current account deficit because uh  we have basically been relying on  financial flows into our  financial sects into our into the city  of london really predominantly  rather than you know creating exports or  renovating and they  allowed us to compete internationally  and obviously that's all kind of  overlain with this  long deep secular crisis of climate  breakdown  and that was the kind of economy that we  had in the period just before the  financial crisis so it was one that  wasn't particularly productive that was  highly unequal  and in which a lot of both individuals  and corporations  were massively overburdened by debt and  this is an interesting theme that we'll  pick up on later this theme of corporate  debt because if the 2008 financial  crisis was largely a crisis of kind of  um personal debt of mortgage debt and  the way in which that was kind of uh  transformed into this  very valuable asset by the financial  system then a big part of the crisis  we're facing now has to do with  corporate debt it has to do with the  debt of kind of ordinary corporations  um because obviously at least partly  because of the low interest rate  environment  you know the combination of very low  interest rates but also  not a very dynamic economy so businesses  could borrow money very very cheaply  but they didn't really have much of an  incentive to invest it productively  because there would be no guarantee that  there will be demand for those products  down the line so  it kind of exacerbated that problem of  stagnation because you're borrowing lots  of money but often you're not using it  for investment you're simply borrowing  money  because it's very cheap and maybe you  can use that to buy property or to buy  back your own shares  and indeed we saw from the big tech  companies especially  then because they're so able to borrow  money so cheaply  they were actually borrowing money from  uh from banks from  investors and using that to buy up  higher yielding bonds  in other companies so they were  effectively acting like banks using  their capacity to borrow very very  cheaply  to gain control of other areas of the  economy and that's the interesting thing  about debt  is that it really is a power relation so  you know for the average person debt is  a kind of  um it's a chain around your leg whereas  for a really big powerful corporation or  for a big investor  that is what allows you to actually  expand your ownership and therefore your  power  over the rest of the economy to continue  watching this video  click the link in the top left or in the  description below  or visit iai.tv for more debates and  talks from the world's leading thinkers  on today's biggest ideas  you

